     2,
     "&gt;&gt; As you can see, the example below ignores that there is more than one subject. What is the best way to turn it in a multilevel model (random intercepts and slopes model)? I think the intuitive way to do it would be indexing alpha and beta for subjects, eg, alpha[subj[n]], but I’m already indexing alpha and beta for conditions. Is there any way I can use two indices so to estimate alpha and beta for each subject and condition? Or can I just add a random intercept term to the equation? Any other solution? Any difference in therm of efficiency? \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "Yes, you can do any of these things, but they result in different models.\r<br>The term you&#39;re looking for in the literature is &quot;interaction&quot;.  If you\r<br>have multiple intercepts floating around, you can quickly run into non-identifiability\r<br>issues.\r<br>\r<br>The best source I know for all of this is Gelman and Hill&#39;s multilevel regression\r<br>book.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob"
    ]
   ]
